const EMPIRE_INFO = {
  "Elamite Confederation": "One of the world's earliest civilisations, the Elamites flourished in southwestern Iran for over two millennia. At their peak they sacked Babylon and carried off the famous Code of Hammurabi stele.",
  "Akkadian Empire": "Founded by Sargon of Akkad around 2334 BCE, the Akkadian Empire is widely regarded as history's first true empire. Sargon's conquests brought the city-states of Sumer and Akkad under a single ruler.",
  "Neo-Sumerian Empire": "The Third Dynasty of Ur (Ur III) represented a Sumerian renaissance, with an elaborate bureaucratic state and monumental ziggurats including the great Ziggurat of Ur.",
  "Assyrian Empire": "The Neo-Assyrian Empire became the most feared military power of its age, perfecting siege warfare and using terror as a deliberate tool of rule. At its height it stretched from Egypt to western Iran.",
  "Babylonian Empire": "Under Hammurabi, Babylon became the leading power and cultural centre of Mesopotamia. His law code, among the earliest and best-preserved known, offers a vivid window into Babylonian society.",
  "Hittite Empire": "The Hittites were ancient Anatolia's dominant power and early workers of iron. After their battle with Egypt at Kadesh in 1274 BCE, the rivals concluded around 1259 BCE the earliest peace treaty surviving in both sides' versions.",
  "Mitanni Empire": "A powerful Hurrian kingdom that dominated northern Mesopotamia and Syria, the Mitanni were renowned chariot warriors who established diplomatic marriages with Egypt's pharaohs.",
  "Chaldean Empire": "The Neo-Babylonian Empire under Nebuchadnezzar II destroyed Jerusalem, raised the Ishtar Gate and made Babylon one of antiquity's greatest cities. It is credited with the legendary Hanging Gardens, whose existence is still debated.",
  "Median Empire": "Often called the first Iranian empire, the Medes joined forces with Babylon to sack Nineveh in 612 BCE and bring down Assyria. They laid the groundwork for later Persian imperial organisation.",
  "Achaemenid Empire": "Founded by Cyrus the Great, the Achaemenid Persian Empire was the largest the world had yet seen, stretching from the Balkans and Egypt to the Indus. It was known for tolerating the religions and customs of its subject peoples.",
  "Egyptian Empire New Kingdom": "The New Kingdom saw Egypt reach the height of its imperial power, with warrior pharaohs like Thutmose III and Ramesses II expanding into Nubia and the Levant.",
  "Kushite Empire": "The Kingdom of Kush outlasted Egypt's own empire, with its kings even ruling Egypt as the 25th Dynasty for nearly a century. Its later capital, Meroë, became a major centre of iron production.",
  "Punic Empire": "Carthage, founded by Phoenician settlers, became one of the ancient Mediterranean's greatest trading powers. Its clash with Rome in three Punic Wars shaped the destiny of Western civilisation.",
  "Garamantian Empire": "A remarkable Saharan empire that dug hundreds of underground channels (foggaras) to tap ancient groundwater, enabling agriculture in the heart of the desert.",
  "Fatimid Caliphate": "The Fatimid Caliphate was an Ismaili Shia dynasty that ruled much of North Africa and founded Cairo as its capital. Its al-Azhar mosque, begun in 970, remains a leading centre of Islamic learning today.",
  "Almohad Caliphate": "A Berber Muslim dynasty that ruled North Africa and Iberia, the Almohads imposed a rigorous religious reform while their court patronised philosophers such as Averroes and Ibn Tufayl.",
  "Almoravid Empire": "A Berber dynasty that arose from religious reformers in the Sahara, the Almoravids united the Maghreb and al-Andalus and checked the Christian advance at the Battle of Sagrajas in 1086.",
  "Ptolemaic Empire": "Founded by one of Alexander's generals, the Ptolemaic dynasty made Alexandria the leading intellectual centre of the Hellenistic world. The Great Library and Cleopatra VII were among its most famous legacies.",
  "Aksumite Empire": "One of antiquity's great trading powers, Aksum controlled the Red Sea trade routes and was among the first states to adopt Christianity. Several of its towering granite stelae, often called obelisks, still stand today.",
  "Ghana Empire": "Often regarded as the first great empire of West Africa, Ghana controlled the trans-Saharan gold and salt trade, becoming fabulously wealthy long before European contact with the region.",
  "Kanem-Bornu Empire": "One of Africa's longest-lasting empires, Kanem-Bornu endured for around a thousand years through successive dynasties, controlling central Saharan trade routes from the Lake Chad basin.",
  "Ethiopian Empire": "The Ethiopian Empire traces its legendary origins to King Solomon, and its victory over Italy at Adwa in 1896 made it a symbol of African independence against colonialism.",
  "Mali Empire": "The Mali Empire produced Mansa Musa, whose 1324 pilgrimage to Mecca was so laden with gold that his spending reportedly depressed the value of gold in Cairo for years.",
  "Benin Empire": "The Kingdom of Benin in modern Nigeria produced the famous Benin Bronzes, among Africa's greatest artistic achievements. Looted by British forces in 1897, they are now held in museums worldwide, and some have been returned.",
  "Songhai Empire": "The largest empire in West African history, Songhai ruled from its capital Gao, while Timbuktu flourished as a renowned centre of trade and Islamic learning.",
  "Oyo Empire": "The Oyo Empire was the dominant Yoruba state and one of West Africa's most powerful kingdoms, with a sophisticated constitutional system that could depose an unpopular ruler.",
  "Jolof Empire": "The Jolof Empire united the Wolof peoples of Senegal in one of West Africa's early confederacies, controlling trade between the coast and the interior.",
  "Kongo Empire": "The Kingdom of Kongo was among Africa's most sophisticated states, with a royal bureaucracy, written records after Portuguese contact, and diplomatic relations with the Vatican.",
  "Mutapa Empire": "Founded around 1430 by a prince linked to the declining Great Zimbabwe, the Mutapa Empire controlled the gold trade between the interior and coastal Swahili, and later Portuguese, merchants.",
  "Luba Empire": "The Luba Empire in central Africa developed a sophisticated system of sacred kingship and memory devices (lukasa boards) to record royal history and cosmology.",
  "Darfur Sultanate": "The Sultanate of Darfur was a powerful Islamic state in western Sudan that controlled trans-Saharan trade routes linking it to Egypt and North Africa. It was finally absorbed into Anglo-Egyptian Sudan in 1916.",
  "Ashanti Empire": "The Ashanti Confederacy of Ghana was renowned for its wealth in gold and the sacred Golden Stool, symbol of the nation's soul. Their resistance to British colonialism was fierce and prolonged.",
  "Rozvi Empire": "Founded on the Zimbabwe plateau in the late 17th century, the Rozvi Empire carried on Great Zimbabwe's stone-building tradition and drove Portuguese traders from the interior.",
  "Lunda Empire": "The Lunda Empire of central Africa created a remarkably flexible political system that expanded by incorporating local rulers, spreading Lunda culture across a vast region.",
  "Adal Sultanate": "The Sultanate of Adal launched a devastating jihad against Christian Ethiopia under Ahmad ibn Ibrahim, nearly extinguishing the ancient Ethiopian Empire.",
  "Kaabu Empire": "The Empire of Kaabu emerged from Mali's western provinces to become a dominant Mandé state in West Africa, controlling coastal trade in modern Guinea-Bissau and Senegal.",
  "Wadai Empire": "The Sultanate of Wadai was a powerful Islamic state in the eastern Sahel, enriched by trans-Saharan trade, that resisted French conquest until its capital Abéché fell in 1909.",
  "Sokoto Caliphate": "Founded by the reformer Usman dan Fodio, the Sokoto Caliphate was one of Africa's largest 19th-century states, a theocratic empire spanning much of modern Nigeria.",
  "Macedonian Empire": "Alexander the Great's eleven-year campaign created one of the largest empires of antiquity, spreading Greek culture from Egypt to India and transforming the ancient world.",
  "Roman Empire": "At its peak, the Roman Empire spanned some five million square kilometres and an estimated 50 to 70 million people. Roman law, language and engineering shaped Western civilisation for two millennia.",
  "Byzantine Empire": "The Eastern Roman Empire survived nearly a thousand years after the fall of Rome in the West, preserving classical knowledge and Orthodox Christianity. Constantinople fell to the Ottomans only in 1453.",
  "Carolingian Empire": "Charlemagne's empire united much of Western Europe for the first time since Rome, launching the Carolingian Renaissance and laying the political foundations of modern France and Germany.",
  "Holy Roman Empire": "Voltaire famously quipped that it was 'neither holy, nor Roman, nor an empire', yet this complex patchwork of mainly German states shaped central European politics for over eight centuries.",
  "Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth": "One of Europe's largest states and an early experiment in constitutional monarchy, the Commonwealth's noble democracy shaped Central European culture and politics.",
  "Swedish Empire": "Sweden's Age of Greatness saw this small Nordic kingdom become a major European power controlling much of the Baltic Sea, before overextension led to collapse at Poltava.",
  "British Empire": "The largest empire in history, covering a quarter of the Earth's land surface, the British Empire shaped global trade, language, law, and culture on an unprecedented scale.",
  "French Empire": "France's imperial ambitions peaked first under Napoleon, who redrew the map of Europe, and again in the late 19th and early 20th centuries, when France built the world's second-largest colonial empire.",
  "Spanish Empire": "Often called the first global empire, Spain conquered the Aztec and Inca states and opened the Americas to European colonisation. Silver from Potosí and Zacatecas financed Habsburg wars and transformed the global economy.",
  "Portuguese Empire": "Portugal, a small kingdom of perhaps a million people, pioneered Europe's Age of Discovery and built one of the first global maritime empires, with footholds from Brazil and West Africa to India and Japan.",
  "Dutch Empire": "In its 17th-century Golden Age this small republic became Europe's leading commercial power, and its East India Company (VOC), with permanent capital and tradable shares, is often called the first multinational corporation.",
  "Italian Empire": "Begun in the 1880s with footholds in Eritrea and Somalia, Italy's colonial empire peaked under Mussolini, who dreamed of a new Roman Mediterranean. It collapsed amid Second World War defeats in East Africa, North Africa and Greece.",
  "Austro-Hungarian Empire": "The Dual Monarchy of 1867–1918 was one of Europe's most ethnically diverse states, home to a dozen or more nationalities. Its break-up after the First World War redrew Central Europe as a patchwork of new nation-states.",
  "German Empire": "Unified in 1871, the Kaiserreich industrialised so rapidly that it became continental Europe's leading industrial power, but its militarism, naval rivalry with Britain and anxious diplomacy helped bring on the First World War.",
  "Russian Empire": "Spanning some 22.8 million square kilometres at its peak, the Russian Empire was one of the largest states in history, often ranked third after the British and Mongol empires. Its collapse in 1917 reshaped world politics for a century.",
  "Brazilian Empire": "Brazil's constitutional monarchy (1822–1889) was the only lasting empire in the Americas and one of 19th-century Latin America's most stable governments. It abolished slavery in 1888, only to fall to a military coup the next year.",
  "Rashidun Caliphate": "Led by the first four caliphs after Muhammad, the Rashidun Caliphate (632–661) conquered Syria, Egypt, Iraq and the Sasanian Persian Empire in one of the fastest expansions in history.",
  "Umayyad Caliphate": "Ruling from Damascus, the Umayyad Caliphate built one of the largest empires the world had yet seen, stretching from Spain to Central Asia and the Indus. It made Arabic the language of government and learning across that vast realm.",
  "Abbasid Caliphate": "The Abbasid golden age brought major advances in mathematics, astronomy, medicine and philosophy, as scholars translated and built on Greek, Persian and Indian learning. Baghdad, home of the famed House of Wisdom, was a great centre of scholarship.",
  "Ayyubid Empire": "Saladin's Ayyubid dynasty recaptured Jerusalem from the Crusaders in 1187, reshaping the balance of power in the medieval Middle East. Saladin himself became a revered hero in both Islamic and European memory.",
  "Mamluk Sultanate": "The Mamluks, former slave soldiers, halted the Mongol advance at Ain Jalut in 1260 and went on to expel the last Crusaders from the Levant. They ruled Egypt and Syria from 1250 until the Ottoman conquest of 1517.",
  "Delhi Sultanate": "Five successive dynasties made the Delhi Sultanate (1206–1526) the dominant power of medieval North India, introducing Persian administrative culture and fostering a syncretic Indo-Islamic civilisation.",
  "Seljuk Empire": "The Seljuk Turks took Baghdad in 1055 as protectors of the Abbasid caliph and crushed the Byzantines at Manzikert in 1071. Their advance into Anatolia prompted the Byzantine appeal for help that launched the First Crusade.",
  "Safavid Empire": "The Safavids made Twelver Shia Islam Persia's state religion from 1501, forging the Shia identity of modern Iran and a lasting sectarian rivalry with the Ottomans. Their capital Isfahan became one of the world's most celebrated cities.",
  "Afsharid Empire": "Nader Shah, sometimes called the Napoleon of Persia, briefly reunited Iran and campaigned from Delhi to the Caucasus. His sack of Delhi in 1739 dealt the Mughal Empire a blow from which it never recovered.",
  "Zand Empire": "The Zand dynasty under Karim Khan gave 18th-century Persia a rare period of relative stability and artistic flourishing, with Shiraz as its capital and cultural centre.",
  "Mughal Empire": "At their height the Mughals ruled most of the Indian subcontinent, fostering a sophisticated syncretic culture. The Taj Mahal, built by Shah Jahan, remains one of the world's most celebrated monuments.",
  "Ottoman Empire": "The Ottoman Empire lasted over six centuries (c. 1299–1922) and at its height ruled across three continents. It developed a brilliant civilisation of its own, from Sinan's mosques to a sophisticated imperial administration.",
  "Ilkhanate": "The Mongol Ilkhanate ruled Persia and Mesopotamia, sacking Baghdad in 1258 before converting to Islam under Ghazan in 1295 and becoming a great patron of Persian art and scholarship.",
  "Khwarazmian Empire": "The Khwarazmian Empire briefly dominated Central Asia before Genghis Khan's devastating invasion, one of history's most catastrophic conquests, largely triggered by Khwarazmian provocation.",
  "Xiongnu Empire": "The Xiongnu were the first great steppe empire to challenge China, raiding and extracting tribute from the Han for centuries. Qin and Han walls were built partly against them, and some scholars link them to the later Huns.",
  "Han Dynasty": "The Han Dynasty (206 BCE-220 CE) shaped Chinese civilisation for two millennia, and China's majority ethnic group still calls itself Han in its honour. The Silk Road opened during Han expansion into Central Asia.",
  "Tang Dynasty": "The Tang Dynasty is often considered China's golden age, when Chang'an, with perhaps a million inhabitants, was among the world's largest cities and a cosmopolitan hub of art, poetry and international trade.",
  "Song Dynasty": "The Song Dynasty introduced the first government-issued paper money, movable-type printing, gunpowder weapons and the magnetic compass at sea, innovations that would transform the world, alongside one of China's most refined cultures.",
  "Ming Empire": "The Ming Dynasty built the Forbidden City and the final Great Wall, restored Chinese sovereignty after Mongol rule, and launched Zheng He's extraordinary naval expeditions.",
  "Qing Empire": "The Manchu Qing was China's last imperial dynasty (1644–1912), expanding China to its greatest territorial extent before declining in the 19th century under Western pressure and vast internal rebellions.",
  "Yuan Dynasty": "Kublai Khan's Yuan Dynasty was the Mongol Empire's Chinese chapter, reunifying China in 1279 and linking it to trade routes across Eurasia. Marco Polo's account of Kublai's court amazed Europe.",
  "Mongol Empire": "The Mongol Empire remains history's largest contiguous land empire. Its Pax Mongolica enabled unprecedented trade and exchange across Eurasia, connecting civilisations from China to Europe.",
  "Chagatai Empire": "The Chagatai Khanate, the Central Asian realm allotted to Genghis Khan's second son, Chagatai, ruled the lands between the Oxus and the Tarim Basin, gradually becoming Muslim and Turkic in culture.",
  "Timurid Empire": "Timur (Tamerlane) built an empire of extraordinary artistic refinement alongside devastating conquest. The Timurid Renaissance in Samarkand and Herat produced some of Islam's greatest art.",
  "Gokturk Empire": "The First Turkic Khaganate established the Turkic peoples as a major force in Eurasian history, creating the first political entity to use the word 'Türk' as an identity.",
  "Khazar Khaganate": "The Khazar Khaganate, the steppe power between Byzantium and the Islamic caliphates, controlled the Volga trade route, and its ruling elite made the remarkable choice to convert to Judaism.",
  "Tokugawa Shogunate": "The Tokugawa shoguns gave Japan over 250 years of internal peace (1603–1868) while tightly restricting foreign contact. The Edo period produced kabuki, ukiyo-e woodblock prints and Basho's hokku, ancestor of the haiku.",
  "Empire of Japan": "Imperial Japan's rapid modernisation after 1868 transformed a feudal, largely isolated state into a major industrial and military power within a single generation.",
  "Maurya Empire": "Under Ashoka the Great the Maurya Empire reached its peak and then transformed, as Ashoka embraced Buddhism after the bloody Kalinga war and his edicts and missions helped carry it beyond India.",
  "Gupta Empire": "The Gupta period is often called India's classical or golden age, when Aryabhata advanced astronomy and mathematics using the decimal place-value system and Kalidasa wrote his celebrated Sanskrit works.",
  "Srivijaya Empire": "Srivijaya was the dominant maritime power of Southeast Asia from the 7th to 13th centuries, controlling the Strait of Malacca and the sea trade between India and China. It was also a renowned centre of Buddhist learning.",
  "Khmer Empire": "The Khmer Empire built Angkor Wat, the world's largest religious monument, at the heart of one of the pre-modern world's largest urban complexes, sustained by a vast network of reservoirs and canals.",
  "Majapahit Empire": "Majapahit was the high point of Hindu-Buddhist Java, a maritime power that claimed suzerainty over much of the modern Indonesian archipelago and remains a symbol of national unity.",
  "Sikh Empire": "The Sikh Empire under Maharaja Ranjit Singh was one of the last major powers to resist British expansion in India, renowned for its disciplined Khalsa army and religious tolerance.",
  "Parthian Empire": "The Parthian Empire held the vital Silk Road between Rome and China, enriching itself as the middleman of Eurasian trade while successfully resisting Roman conquest for centuries.",
  "Sasanian Empire": "The last great pre-Islamic Persian empire, the Sasanians rivalled Rome and Byzantium for four centuries. Their art and administrative systems profoundly influenced early Islamic civilisation.",
  "Seleucid Empire": "Founded by Seleucus, one of Alexander's generals, the Seleucid Empire spread Greek culture from Anatolia to Central Asia. Its attempt to suppress Jewish practice provoked the Maccabean revolt, remembered at Hanukkah.",
  "Maratha Empire": "Rising from Shivaji's hill forts and guerrilla warfare, the Maratha Empire broke Mughal supremacy and dominated 18th-century India. Defeat at Panipat in 1761 and the Anglo-Maratha Wars (1775–1818) ended its independence.",
  "Aztec Empire": "From their island capital Tenochtitlan, perhaps 200,000 strong and among the largest cities on earth in 1519, the Aztec Triple Alliance ruled much of central Mexico. Its art, ritual and grandeur astonished the Spanish conquistadors."
};

const EVENTS = [
  {year: -776, label: "First Olympics", desc: "The ancient Olympic Games were held at Olympia in Greece for the first time, inaugurating a sacred truce among warring city-states and a tradition of athletic competition that would endure for over a millennium."},
  {year: -509, label: "Roman Republic Founded", desc: "Rome expelled its last king, Tarquinius Superbus, and established a republican government. The Senate, consuls, and assemblies it created would shape Western political thought for two thousand years."},
  {year: -330, label: "Alexander's Empire", desc: "Alexander the Great completed his conquest of the Achaemenid Persian Empire, reaching from Macedonia to the borders of India. His campaigns spread Greek language and culture across the ancient world in what we now call the Hellenistic Age."},
  {year: -221, label: "Qin Unifies China", desc: "Qin Shi Huang unified the Warring States, becoming the first Emperor of a united China. He standardized weights, measures, writing, and coinage — and began construction of the Great Wall."},
  {year: -44, label: "Caesar Assassinated", desc: "Julius Caesar was assassinated on the Ides of March by a group of senators led by Brutus and Cassius. His death plunged Rome into civil war and ultimately led to the end of the Republic and the rise of the Roman Empire."},
  {year: 79, label: "Vesuvius Erupts", desc: "Mount Vesuvius erupted catastrophically, burying the Roman cities of Pompeii and Herculaneum under meters of volcanic ash. The preserved ruins have provided an unparalleled window into daily life in the ancient Roman world."},
  {year: 476, label: "Fall of W. Rome", desc: "The Germanic chieftain Odoacer deposed Romulus Augustulus, the last Western Roman Emperor. Traditionally marking the end of Antiquity and the beginning of the Middle Ages in Europe."},
  {year: 570, label: "Birth of Muhammad", desc: "The Prophet Muhammad was born in Mecca, Arabia. His revelations, compiled as the Quran, would give rise to Islam — a faith that within a century would unite the Arabian Peninsula and reshape the political map of three continents."},
  {year: 632, label: "Islamic Expansion", desc: "Following the death of Muhammad, the Rashidun Caliphate launched a series of rapid conquests. Within a generation, Arab armies had subdued Persia, the Levant, Egypt, and North Africa in one of history's most dramatic imperial expansions."},
  {year: 800, label: "Charlemagne Crowned", desc: "Pope Leo III crowned Charlemagne Emperor of the Romans on Christmas Day in Rome. This coronation revived the idea of a Western Empire, laid the foundations of medieval Europe, and shaped the political geography of France and Germany."},
  {year: 1066, label: "Norman Conquest", desc: "William the Conqueror defeated King Harold II at the Battle of Hastings, fundamentally transforming England. The Norman Conquest reshaped English language, law, culture, and aristocracy in ways that persist to the present day."},
  {year: 1206, label: "Genghis Khan", desc: "Temüjin was proclaimed Genghis Khan — Universal Ruler — at a grand assembly of Mongol tribes. He then launched a series of conquests that would create history's largest contiguous land empire, forever altering Eurasia's political landscape."},
  {year: 1347, label: "Black Death", desc: "The bubonic plague reached Sicily from Central Asia, spreading across Europe with devastating speed. Within five years it had killed an estimated one-third of Europe's population, triggering profound social, economic, and religious upheaval."},
  {year: 1453, label: "Fall of Constantinople", desc: "Ottoman Sultan Mehmed II conquered Constantinople after a 53-day siege, ending the Byzantine Empire and the last remnant of Rome. The fall accelerated the Renaissance as Greek scholars fled west, and marked a new era of Ottoman dominance."},
  {year: 1492, label: "Columbus Voyage", desc: "Christopher Columbus, sailing for the Spanish Crown, reached the Americas. His voyage initiated sustained contact between the Old and New Worlds, leading to the Columbian Exchange and the era of European colonial empires."},
  {year: 1517, label: "Reformation", desc: "Martin Luther published his Ninety-Five Theses challenging papal authority and the sale of indulgences. His protest sparked the Protestant Reformation, permanently fracturing Western Christendom and reshaping European politics for centuries."},
  {year: 1648, label: "Peace of Westphalia", desc: "The treaties ending the Thirty Years' War established the modern concept of state sovereignty and non-interference in internal affairs. The Westphalian system remains the foundational framework of international relations."},
  {year: 1776, label: "American Independence", desc: "The thirteen American colonies declared independence from Britain, articulating Enlightenment ideals of liberty and self-governance. The United States that followed became a model for republican government worldwide."},
  {year: 1789, label: "French Revolution", desc: "The French Revolution overthrew the monarchy and swept away the old aristocratic order. Its ideals of liberté, égalité, fraternité — and the terror that followed — sent shockwaves through every royal court in Europe."},
  {year: 1815, label: "Congress of Vienna", desc: "After Napoleon's defeat, European powers met in Vienna to redraw the map of Europe. The conservative order they established maintained relative peace for nearly a century, reshaping borders and balance of power."},
  {year: 1914, label: "WWI Begins", desc: "The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand triggered a chain of alliances that engulfed Europe in a catastrophic war. The Great War killed 20 million people, destroyed four empires, and set the conditions for an even deadlier conflict."},
  {year: 1945, label: "WWII Ends", desc: "World War II ended with the defeat of Nazi Germany and Imperial Japan. The war's aftermath — the United Nations, decolonization, the Cold War, and the atomic age — defined the second half of the twentieth century."},
];

